       " Document(metadata={'parent_asin': '1429272406', 'average_rating': 3.5}, page_content=\"Classical Mechanics Paperback – January 1, 2011 {'avatar': 'https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/01Kv-W2ysOL._SY600_.png', 'name': 'Gary Gladding', 'about': ['Discover more of the author’s books, see similar authors, read author blogs and more']} author (Author) Books Science & Math Physics  Smart Physics: Classical Methods by Gary Gladding, Mats Selen and Tim Stelzer\"),\n",
       " Document(metadata={'parent_asin': '0521686970', 'average_rating': 4.2}, page_content=\"The Cambridge Companion to Thomas Jefferson (Cambridge Companions to American Studies) First Edition None Frank Shuffelton (Editor) Books Biographies & Memoirs Leaders & Notable People Review '… the essays focusing on Jefferson-the-man help us understand the inner character and soul of the prominent 'founding father' of America …' The Oxonian Book Description An accessible introduction to the life and work of Jefferson aimed at students of American history and literature. Book Description An accessible introduction to the life and work of Thomas Jefferson. Contributors include leading literary scholars and historians. This Companion will be essential reading for students and scholars of Jefferson, and is designed for use by students of American literature and American history. About the Author Frank Shuffelton is Professor of English at the University of Rochester, New York. Read more This Companion forms an accessible introduction to the life and work of Thomas Jefferson, third President of the United States and author of the Declaration of Independence. Essays explore Jefferson's political thought, his policies towards Native Americans, his attitude to race and slavery, as well as his interests in science, architecture, religion and education. Contributors include leading literary scholars and historians; the essays offer up to date overviews of his many interests, his friendships and his legacy. Together, they reveal his importance in the cultural and political life of early America. At the same time these original essays speak to abiding modern concerns about American culture and Jefferson's place in it. This Companion will be essential reading for students and scholars of Jefferson, and is designed for use by students of American literature and American history.\"),\n",
